comments:
An alternative suggestion is to split things up and start to use independent IDs (current IDs preserved for backwards compadibility):

* Split `KestrelTrace` into multiple partial type files, based on category (i.e. general, connection, HTTP/2, HTTP/3).
* Each partial type file has its own private static type. Log generation happens on this type. This is required because log generation validates that event IDs aren't duplicated. The `KestrelTrace` partial type files are all one type from log generation's perspective so log generation can't happen on it anymore.
* Each category starts using independent event IDs. All new log IDs for each category start from the current highest number, e.g. 60.

e.g.

KestrelTrace.Connections.cs:
```cs
internal sealed partial class KestrelTrace : ILogger
{
    public void ConnectionAccepted(string connectionId)
    {
        ConnectionsLog.ConnectionAccepted(_connectionsLogger, connectionId);
    }

    private partial static class ConnectionsLog
    {
        [LoggerMessage(39, LogLevel.Debug, @"Connection id ""{ConnectionId}"" accepted.", EventName = "ConnectionAccepted")]
        private static partial void ConnectionAccepted(ILogger logger, string connectionId);
    }
}
```

Repeat for general, HTTP/2, HTTP/3, etc.

Note that partial files will be able to see and access `ILogger` and static log types for other categories. e.g. `KestrelTrace.Connections.cs` could use `_generalLogger` and the `GeneralLog` static type. I don't see a way around this without breaking up KestrelTrace itself. The simple solution is don't use them 😄

